{
 "paper": "2608.25490v1",
 "writer": "anthropic/claude-opus-5.5",
 "prompt_version": "p9",
 "insight": {
  "insight": "Existing multimodal jailbreak benchmarks bundle four things into each fixed prompt-image instance: harmful intent, prompt framing, visual semantics and instruction carrier. Because of this, their ASR shows only aggregate vulnerability. MMJailBench factorizes instances into these four components and takes their full Cartesian product over matched intents (272\u00d76\u00d75\u00d72 = 16,320 per model). Holding the intent fixed while varying one factor at a time allows controlled comparisons that attribute vulnerability to individual factors.",
  "dependent_results": [
   "Prompt framing is the dominant source of variation. The gap between the most and least vulnerable framing strategies exceeds 40 points, with story, structured and academic framings higher and system and paradox framings lower. Per-model \u0394Prompt reaches up to 90.8 (qwen2.5-vl-7b) (5.2 Prompt Framing; Table 4; Table 9).",
   "Task-relevant visual semantics raise ASR relative to the no-image baseline of 34.77%: authorization document +12.96, identity credential +10.47, task scenario +10.10. Non-semantic controls (blank, noise, nature images) change ASR by less than 2.2 points, so the increase comes from visual meaning rather than image presence (5.2 Visual Semantics; Table 2).",
   "The OCR carrier gives lower ASR than the TEXT carrier: 40.59% vs 50.70%, and CASR 42.34% vs 50.89% after excluding mismatches (4.14% vs 0.37%). This leads to the conclusion that visually rendered instructions are not inherently stronger and that carrier effects depend on the model (5.2 Instruction Carrier; Table 3).",
   "Vulnerability is uneven across harm domains: cyber, economic, privacy and deception are higher, while sensitive-advice and sexual content are lower. Per-model average ASR ranges from 2.17% (GPT-5) to 78.38% (GLM-4.6V) (5.1; Table 1; Table 8).",
   "Model-dependent profiles: per-model factor ranges (\u0394Intent, \u0394Prompt, \u0394Visual, \u0394Carrier) differ across models, and K-means clusters the models into prompt-dominant, visual-responsive, carrier-sensitive and low-variation groups (5.3; Table 4; C.2).",
   "In gemma3-12b, authority-document contexts diverge from danger contexts in representation space, most sharply at layer 42. An authority-minus-danger direction generalizes to held-out pairs and shifts consistently across all harm domains. At that layer, attention to harm-related task-label tokens and visual tokens is lower (6.1, 6.2)."
  ],
  "key_definitions": [
   "Factorized instance x_{h,t,v,c} = (L(h,t,c), V(h,v)). The instruction text depends on intent h, framing t and carrier c; the image depends on intent h and visual semantics v (3.1 Factorized Design).",
   "Harmful intent: an intent-level behavioral seed (272 total, 9 domains, 18 scenarios), kept fixed while the contextual factors vary (3.2; A.1).",
   "Prompt framing: 6 templates (academic, system, story, code, structured, safety-paradox) that change the linguistic presentation while preserving intent (3.3; A.2).",
   "Visual semantics: 5 task-relevant image conditions (danger, scenario, professional role, identity credential, authorization document), plus control conditions (no image, blank, noise, nature) used in the ablation (3.4; A.3).",
   "Instruction carrier: TEXT (instruction given directly as text) vs OCR (instruction rendered inside an image) (3.5; A.4).",
   "ASR: the fraction of responses with judge harmfulness score s \u2265 4 on a 1\u20135 rubric, where 4 means actionable harmful assistance (4.2; Table 7).",
   "CASR = N_success / (N \u2212 N_mismatch). Mismatch covers OCR recognition failures, irrelevant responses and misinterpretation of the intent (4.2).",
   "\u0394 factor score: the range of ASR across the configurations of a given factor for a given model (Table 4, 5.3)."
  ],
  "reasoning_steps": [
   "Entangled fixed instances cannot show which factor causes an observed vulnerability, which motivates controlled factorization (1 Introduction; 3.1).",
   "Each intent is crossed with every framing, visual condition and carrier, giving 16,320 instances per model. Every model is run on all of them, for 261,120 responses across 16 MLLMs (3.1; 4.1; B.1).",
   "Responses are scored by a GPT-5 judge, validated against human experts (QWK 0.97). ASR is computed from these scores, and CASR is used to separate perception failures from safety behavior (4.2; C.4 Table 12).",
   "Marginalizing over the other factors yields per-factor ASR. Comparing factor ranges shows that framing variation (>40 points) far exceeds visual variation (~13 points) (5.2; Table 4).",
   "Comparing semantic images against no-image and non-semantic control images isolates the effect of visual meaning from the mere presence of an image (5.2 Visual Semantics; Table 2).",
   "Conditioning on correct instruction understanding via CASR shows that the TEXT>OCR gap is not caused by OCR misreading (5.2 Instruction Carrier; Table 3).",
   "Matched authority vs danger instances are used to probe gemma3-12b's residual stream and attention. These analyses link the authority-cue behavioral effect to shifts in higher-layer representations and redistributed attention (6.1, 6.2).",
   "A stratified 1,500-instance lightweight configuration and a distilled qwen3-8b judge reproduce the full results: Spearman \u03c1 0.997 against the full configuration, and QWK 0.95 against GPT-5. This supports the claim of cheap, reproducible auditing (B.3; B.4; C.4 Tables 11-12)."
  ]
 },
